# Meter — a level inside a range, with a tone: disk used, seats taken, budget
# spent, rate limit remaining. It is `ProgressBar`'s twin and the difference is
# what the bar MEANS.
#
#     use Meter("Storage", 41, 50, "warn")
#     use Meter("Seats", 3, 25, "ok")
#
# A PROGRESS BAR MEASURES A TASK AND ONLY EVER GOES UP; A METER MEASURES A STATE
# AND CAN GO EITHER WAY. That is why a progress bar is always the accent colour
# (it is neutral — an import is not good or bad news, it is just underway) and a
# meter is toned: 41 of 50 is a warning if it is storage and unremarkable if it is
# a word count, and only the caller knows which.
#
# The tone is one of the four `ui/alert.fct` defines and is applied as an
# interpolated class, so an unknown tone falls through to the accent rather than
# failing. The fill vocabulary — the fifty-one width classes and the reason there
# have to be fifty-one of them — is `ui/progress.fct`'s, imported rather than
# repeated; the note there is worth reading before adding a third bar of any kind
# to this library.
#
# THE READOUT IS THE VALUE AND ITS MAXIMUM, NOT A PERCENTAGE. A meter's units are
# the thing being measured ("41 / 50 GB" is the sentence the caller wants), and a
# percentage throws away the half of that the reader needs. `ProgressBar` shows a
# percentage for the opposite reason: the units of a task are steps nobody counts.
import "progress.fct"
import "alert.fct"
app FacetUI:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-meter { --x-tone: var(--fa-accent); }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-meter .x-meter-fill { background: var(--x-tone); }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-meter .x-progress-track { height: 10px; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-meter-read { display: block; color: var(--fa-muted);
                       font-size: 13px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
    component Meter(label: text, value: int, max: int, tone: text):
        box class "x-progress x-meter x-tone-{tone}":
            row class "x-progress-head":
                if label != "":
                    text "{label}" class "x-progress-label"
                if max > 0:
                    text "{value} / {max}" class "x-meter-read"
            if max > 0:
                box class "x-progress-track":
                    box class "x-progress-fill x-meter-fill x-fill-{value * 50 / max}"
